response:
 The length of the common chord of two overlapping circles can be calculated using the geometry of intersecting circles. When two circles of equal radii intersect such that each circle passes through the center of the other, the shape formed by the intersection is called a symmetric lens or a vesica piscis. The length of the common chord of the two circles is equal to the height of the equilateral triangle formed by the centers of the circles minus the side length of the equilateral triangle. Since the radii of the circles are equal and each circle passes through the center of the other, the distance between the centers of the two circles is equal to the sum of the radii minus the side length of the equilateral triangle, which in this case is the same as the radius of the circles, \(10\) cm. Therefore, the length of the common chord can be calculated as follows: \[
\text{Length of the common chord} = 2 \times \left( \text{radius} - \text{height of the equilateral triangle} \right) = 2 \times \left( 10 - \frac{10\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) = 2 \times \left( 10 - 5\sqrt{3} \right) = 20 - 10\sqrt{3} \text{ cm.}
\] </think> <answer> $20 - 10\sqrt{3}$ </answer>

